apply must leave everything about a file as it was except its content and its name. Today it does not:
- OWNER/GROUP: a rewritten file is a new file (temp file renamed into place, since k12), so it belongs to whoever runs codefork. The documented Docker usage (docker run -v "$PWD:/work" raudssus/codefork apply) runs as root in the container, so every rewritten file on the host ends up owned by root. That is the normal case, not an edge case.
- HARD LINKS: the rename replaces the inode, so a second hard link silently keeps the old content and the two names are no longer the same file.
- DIRECTORIES: for a renamed directory, apply mkpaths the new directory (owner = runner, mode = umask) and moves the files one by one; owner and mode of the original directory are lost.

Work (test each, failing first, own temp tree):
- keep owner and group of a rewritten file: chown the temp file to the old uid/gid before the rename. When that is not permitted (not root, file owned by someone else) or the file has more than one hard link (nlink > 1), write the new content into the existing inode instead (open read-write, truncate, write) - that keeps owner, group, mode and links. Renamed+modified files get the same treatment, then the rename is a move of that inode.
- a directory created for a rename takes mode and (when permitted) owner/group of the directory it replaces.
- tests: hard link survives (both names show the new content, same inode); mode and group preserved; the chown path can only be fully tested as root - test it when $> == 0, skip otherwise with a reason, and test the fallback path by forcing it.
- document in POD/README: apply keeps owner, group, mode and hard links.

DESIGN DECISION (maintainer): write into the existing inode with a backup copy for crash safety - copy old file to a 0600 backup in the same dir, truncate+write the original, remove the backup only on success, restore from it on failure. Renamed+modified: write in place, then rename(2). Replaces the chown/fallback plan above; keeps owner, group, mode, hard links, ACLs, xattrs by construction. Directory owner/mode on rename stays in scope.

